  "context_pill": "Corporate PGBP Computation",
  "example_summary": "Alpha Ltd's PGBP computation involving cash disallowances, current repairs, cessation of liability, and scientific research deductions for AY 2026-27.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Summary Matrix: Corporate PGBP Adjustments\n\n| Area / Head | Statutory Section / Rule | Operative Threshold / Condition | Tax Treatment / Legal Consequence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Related Party Payment | Section 40A(2) | Excessive or unreasonable payments to directors or relatives | Disallowance of the excess portion over market value |\n| Cash Payments | Section 40A(3) | Aggregate cash payment exceeding ₹10,000 to a person in a single day | 100% disallowance of the cash expenditure amount |\n| Current Repairs | Section 31 | Expenditure on replacement of worn-out parts not bringing a new asset | Fully allowed as revenue expenditure if no enduring benefit |\n| Cessation of Liability | Section 41(1) | Remission or cessation of a trading liability previously allowed as deduction | Deemed business income in the year of cessation |\n| Export Incentives | Section 28(iiia) | Sale of import entitlements or licenses granted under export schemes | Chargeable as business income under PGBP |\n| Scientific Research | Section 35(2AB) | Capital and revenue expenditure on in-house R&D by approved biotech companies | 100% deduction under Section 35(2AB) |\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Disallowance Interplay:\n> Section 40A(3) applies independently of Section 40A(2). When a cash payment exceeds the ₹10,000 threshold, the entire qualifying sum faces complete disallowance regardless of market value reasonableness.\n\n\n> [!NOTE] Tangible Fixed Asset ICDS V Benchmark:\n> Under ICDS V, only expenditure that increases future benefits from an existing asset beyond its previously assessed standard of performance is capitalized to actual cost.",
